g margins and carrying a partisan lean of roughly five points toward the GOP. With the incumbent term-limited, the 2028 race is open on both sides, and no general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures are yet available for the state.\n\nThat leaves fundamentals to carry the forecast. Headlines point to Democratic optimism in Ohio's 2026 races, including Sherrod Brown's lead in Senate betting markets, and to speculation about home-state Republicans like JD Vance or Vivek Ramaswamy topping the 2028 ticket. Neither development is enough to move the race far from its Republican lean. The state starts as Lean Republican, with wide uncertainty until real polling arrives.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2797, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3055, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.299, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3175, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3176, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3303, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3186, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3187, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.3183, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.3186, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.3337, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.3099, "margin": -5.0}]}